{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true,
    "strict": false,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "outDir": "./dist",
    // The PACKAGE root, not `./src`, because `include` reaches `scripts/**` too
    // and `rootDir` is a constraint on every file in the program: with `./src`
    // this configuration reports ten TS6059s and typechecks nothing under
    // `scripts/`. Nothing emits from `tsc` here — `typecheck` is `tsc --noEmit`
    // and `build` is esbuild (`build.ts`), which computes its own base from its
    // three entry points — so `outDir` and `rootDir` describe no artefact
    // anybody ships and widening the second changes no output.
    //
    // `packages/`, not the package root, since #1017: the SDK contract suite
    // (`src/routes/__tests__/public-api-sdk-contract.realdb.test.ts`) imports
    // `@mercaria.co/sdk` from its SOURCE through `paths` below, and a `.ts`
    // file outside `rootDir` is a TS6059 that refuses the whole program — at the
    // package root it reported one per SDK module. Still no output, for the
    // reason above.
    "rootDir": "..",
    // The published SDK, from SOURCE, for the contract suite only.
    // `vitest.config.ts` READS this entry and applies it as its runtime alias,
    // so the two cannot name different files. Its `package.json` exports
    // point at `dist/`, which does not exist until the SDK is built, and a
    // contract test against a stale build would prove the wrong artefact. The
    // SDK's own strict, no-DOM program is `packages/sdk/tsconfig.json`; here it
    // is only an import — but it is compiled under THIS program's options, so
    // SDK source must also type-check with `strict: false` (a
    // `x !== null && typeof x !== …` narrowing needs `strictNullChecks`; see
    // `nullableRating` in `packages/sdk/src/parse.ts`). No production module
    // imports it.
    "paths": {
      "@mercaria.co/sdk": ["../sdk/src/index.ts"]
    },
    "declaration": false,
    "sourceMap": false,
    "types": ["node"],
    "incremental": true,
    "isolatedModules": true
  },
  // @mercaria/shared-types resolves through the workspace symlink to its
  // built declarations (packages/shared-types/dist/index.d.ts, produced by the
  // root postinstall). Declaration files are exempt from `rootDir`, so no path
  // alias is needed here and the package's compiled `src/**` is not pulled into
  // this program. The runtime build inlines it via esbuild (see build.ts).
  //
  // `scripts/**` is here as well as `src/**`, and it is the whole of #374: with
  // `src` alone, `tsc --noEmit` honoured that `include` and a green CI said
  // NOTHING about any file under `scripts/` — which is the structural reason
  // #299's divergent second copy of the Shopify admin client could sit there for
  // months. `scripts/` holds the #69 real-store E2E driver, the graph benchmark
  // and the offer load benchmark; a driver whose job is to report whether an
  // integration works is exactly where an unnoticed type error is expensive.
  //
  // `build.ts` is named individually, and it is the one package-root file that
  // earns it: it PRODUCES the production image's `dist/`, so ungated it is the
  // only file here whose type error could ship a broken artefact — a stronger
  // claim than anything under `scripts/`. `drizzle.config.ts` and the three
  // `vitest*.ts` are deliberately still out: they configure dev-time tools that
  // fail loudly and locally when they are wrong, so the argument does not reach
  // them. It costs two files (`build.ts` plus `esbuild`'s own `main.d.ts`, and
  // esbuild is already a dependency), measured with `--listFiles`: 3326 → 3328.
  "include": ["src/**/*", "scripts/**/*", "build.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist"]
}
